E.1 General
Industrial truck manufacturers as original equipment manufacturers, as well as suppliers of truck equipment are increasingly offering systems that assist the operator in the operation of industrial trucks. Some kinds of assistance systems can improve the operational performances and/or the ergonomics of industrial trucks while others can reduce residual risks e.g. caused by improper use or inattentiveness of the operator. Examples of assistance systems are:
� visual and/or audible warning devices that warn of movements of trucks;
� collision warning devices that warn the operator when approaching obstacles;
� systems limiting the truck performance or LHS functions in defined areas or use conditions;
� data terminals.

C.2.6.2 Type plate
The identification plate shall be durably fixed on the battery, shall be in accordance with EN 62620:2015, Clause 5 and in accordance with EN IEC 62902:2019. Additionally, the following information shall be given on the identification plate:
� stored energy in Wh;
� weight of the battery;
� warning to avoid external heat sources.

E.2.3 Reliability
Assistance systems shall be designed to withstand the expected operating stresses and relevant external influences, e.g. mechanical vibration, electromagnetic interference, power supply interruptions or disturbances.
Assistance functions shall be designed in accordance with the applicable clauses of this document.
Assistance functions with the purpose to reduce residual risks (e.g. caused by improper use or inattentiveness of the operator) shall additionally be designed using:
� parts widely utilized with successful results in similar applications;
� parts designed using principles of EN ISO 13849-1:2015 demonstrating its suitability and reliability;
� complex electronic circuits (e.g. containing microprocessors, application-specific integrated circuits) based on components and architectures suitable for safety-relevant applications or designed in accordance with good engineering practices and the state of the art;
in any combination.

C.2.6.3 Instruction handbook
Lithium-ion batteries shall be supplied to the user with an instruction handbook, covering operating and regular servicing and addressing all identified hazards.
NOTE The instruction handbook can be integrated in the truck handbook.

The instruction handbook(s) shall include, as applicable, at least the following information:
a) name and address of the manufacturer or authorized representative;
b) designation of type;
c) description of the battery, suitable chargers and accessories;
d) description of safety devices and warning labels;
e) intended uses of the system, and examples of hazardous misuse and limits of interoperability across different technologies and voltage/current ranges;
f) training requirements for the operator;
g) battery charging procedures and instructions;
h) function of operating controls and displays;
i) pre-shift checks before the system is put into operation;
j) safe procedure for the identification, detection and correction of faults;
k) climatic conditions in which the system is designed to operate;
l) information or instructions on action to be taken in the event of a malfunction;
m) procedure for safe handling of the battery, including installation, removal and secure mounting on the truck;
n) service mass and centre of gravity of battery including all additional parts, e.g. battery, housing, ballast and BMS provided as subassembly;
o) use of approved spare parts;
p) procedures for transporting, including loading and unloading;
q) procedure for transport of inoperative batteries;
r) instructions for disposing of the battery.

4.5.10 Speed limitation
The electrical system limiting truck speed shall be so designed that the admissible maximum speed on level ground is not exceeded. In the event of an electrical fault the transition to the safe state, as defined in 4.5.1 a) and c) shall be initiated.
The following speed limitation functions shall be in accordance with the PLr as defined in Table 2:
a) lateral- and front-stacking trucks in accordance with EN ISO 3691-3:2016, 4.4.1;
b) travel speed limitation to comply with braking and stability requirements, e.g. trucks specifically designed to travel with elevated loads;
c) reach trucks with elevated mast in accordance with EN ISO 3691-1:2015, 4.2.3.3;
d) speed limit of counterbalance trucks to ensure dynamic stability in accordance with EN 16307-1:2013+A1:2015, 4.11;
e) pedestrian controlled trucks in accordance with EN 16307-1:2013+A1:2015, 4.3;
f) if triggered by platform and/or side guards� position in accordance with EN ISO 3691-1:2015, 4.7.3.3;
g) operating from outside the truck in accordance with EN ISO 3691-1:2015, 4.4.2.6;
h) trucks with attachments for freight containers in accordance with EN ISO 3691-1:2015, 4.6.5.5;
i) stand-on and foldable platform trucks in accordance with EN 16307-1:2013+A1:2015, 4.3.
NOTE See Annex E for speed limitation as operator assistance.

